{"step_index":331,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-07T06:33:42Z","input_tokens":3219,"cache_read_tokens":35384,"output_tokens":4326,"thinking":"Here's the plan for `docs/CODE_EXPLANATION.md`: It will detail the core components. The breakdown will start with the Threat Detection Engine and its classes. Specifically, I'll elaborate on the Masquerading algorithm, the command-line heuristic evaluation, and the process scanning pipeline.\n\n\nThe document will now outline heuristic scoring for threat levels and PE forensics, focusing on entropy calculations and packed code detection. It will then detail LOLRMM and dual-use remote access detection using port matching and binary hashing, differentiating legitimate IT tools from malicious ones. Next, access control and the policy engine, including allowlist/blocklist evaluation and Windows Firewall integration, will be described.\n\nThe focus will also include the Remediation Engine's graceful termination vs. forced kernel kill, file quarantine, and audit logging. Network origin tracing, encompassing socket retrieval and reverse DNS queries, along with classification rules for network traffic, will be next. Finally, the AdBlocker Proxy, with its multi-threaded handling, subdomain matching, and HTTP 403 Forbidden responses, will be explained, and the Real component will also be included.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_file","args":{"Content":"\"# AegisShield EDR & Network AdBlocker: Comprehensive Code Explanation\\n\\n## 1. Threat Detection Engine (`backend/detector.py`)\\n\\n### 1.1 Class Architecture: `ThreatDetector`\\nThe `ThreatDetector` class is the central analysis orchestrator for all running processes. It accepts an optional `PolicyEngine` instance to evaluate allowlist and blocklist rules before executing computational heuristics.\\n\\n### 1.2 Masquerading Detection Heuristic\\nAttackers frequently name malware after critical Windows binaries to blend into Task Manager (e.g., placing `svchost.exe` in `%APPDATA%`).\\n```python\\nCRITICAL_WINDOWS_BINARIES = {\\n    \\\"svchost.exe\\\": [\\\"system32\\\", \\\"syswow64\\\"],\\n    \\\"csrss.exe\\\": [\\\"system32\\\"],\\n    \\\"lsass.exe\\\": [\\\"system32\\\"],\\n    \\\"services.exe\\\": [\\\"system32\\\"],\\n    \\\"smss.exe\\\": [\\\"system32\\\"],\\n    \\\"explorer.exe\\\": [\\\"windows\\\"],\\n    \\\"wininit.exe\\\": [\\\"system32\\\"],\\n    \\\"winlogon.exe\\\": [\\\"system32\\\"]\\n}\\n```\\nThe method `is_masquerading_system_binary(proc_name, exe_path)` performs the following validation:\\n1. Normalizes the executable name to lowercase.\\n2. If `proc_name` exists in `CRITICAL_WINDOWS_BINARIES`, it inspects the directory path of `exe_path`.\\n3. If none of the approved system directory substrings (e.g. `C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32`) appear in `exe_path`, the process is flagged as **CRITICAL MASQUERADING** (+35 score penalty).\\n\\n### 1.3 Command-Line Heuristic Scoring\\nThe method `evaluate_cmdline(cmdline_str)` searches for indicators of living-off-the-land binaries (LOLBins) and script execution attacks:\\n- **Base64 / Encoded Commands:** `-enc`, `-encodedcommand`, `frombase64string`.\\n- **Evasion & Hidden Windows:** `-w hidden`, `-windowstyle hidden`, `-ep bypass`, `-executionpolicy bypass`.\\n- **Download Cradles & In-Memory Execution:** `iex`, `invoke-expression`, `downloadstring`, `webclient`, `bitsadmin`, `certutil -urlcache`.\\n- **Credential Access & Memory Dumping:** `procdump`, `comsvcs.dll`, `mimikatz`, `sekurlsa`.\\n- Scoring: Each matched pattern adds +15 to +35 to the thr\n<truncated 10614 bytes>","Path":"\"c:\\\\Users\\\\thann\\\\Downloads\\\\AntiGravity\\\\SpywareProcessMon\\\\docs\\\\CODE_EXPLANATION.md\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
