{"step_index":338,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-07T06:35:02Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-07T07:35:02+01:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-07T07:35:02+01:00\nShowing lines 1-100 of 163\n1: # 🛡️ AegisShield EDR - Cyber Process Monitor & Threat Eradicator\n2: \n3: > **Advanced Real-Time Spyware, Antivirus, Remote Desktop Control (LOLRMM), Process Monitor, Network Origin Tracer, Access Control & Trojan Detection Dashboard**\n4: > *Scans your system, identifies threats via multi-vector heuristics and threat intelligence, audits network connections, enforces allow/block rules, dispatches email reports, and kills threats with a single click.*\n5: \n6: ---\n7: \n8: ## 🌟 Key Features\n9: \n10: ### 1. 🛡️ Access Control & Allow / Block Policy Engine\n11: * **Allowlist (Whitelist):** Explicitly trust processes by **Name**, **Executable Path**, or **SHA256 Hash**. Whitelisted software is assigned a Threat Score of 0, alerts are suppressed, and it is exempt from termination.\n12: * **Blocklist (Blacklist):** Ban known malicious processes or scripts. Any process matching a blacklist rule is flagged as `CRITICAL` (Score: 100) and can be immediately eradicated.\n13: * **IP Banning & Windows Firewall Integration:** Ban suspicious remote IP addresses. AegisShield creates outbound rules in Windows Firewall (`netsh advfirewall firewall add rule ...`) and flags any process attempting communication with banned IPs.\n14: * **Quick UI Actions:** 1-click **Allow** and **Block** buttons located on every process row and threat card.\n15: \n16: ### 2. 🌐 Network Radar & IP Origin Tracer\n17: * **Network Process Auditing:** Discovers exactly which processes are using network sockets, mapping local ports to remote IP endpoints.\n18: * **Origin & Geolocation Tracing:** Resolves remote IPs using reverse DNS (PTR records) and geographic lookup (Country, Organization, ISP, and ASN).\n19: * **Genuineness & Reputation Classification:** Automatically assesses whether an endpoint is legitimate:\n20:   * 🟢 **`GENUINE CLOUD / CDN`**: Verified major tech infrastructure (Microsoft/Azure, Google/GCP, Cloudflare, Amazon AWS, Apple, Akam\n<truncated 3372 bytes>\n, TightVNC on ports 5900 - 5905)\n57:   * **Splashtop**, **LogMeIn**, **Atera**, **NinjaRMM**, **NetSupport Manager**, **Ammyy Admin**, and **Remote Utilities**.\n58: * **Reverse Tunnels & C2 Proxies:** Detects persistent reverse tunnels often abused by attackers (**Ngrok**, **Cloudflared**, **FRP**, **Chisel**, **Ligolo**).\n59: \n60: ### 7. ⚡ Threat Eradication (\"Finds it and Kills it\")\n61: * **Instant Process Termination:** Forcefully terminates rogue processes (`proc.kill()`).\n62: * **Recursive Tree Eradication:** Kills parent processes and all spawned child processes to prevent surviving beacons.\n63: * **🚨 Emergency Bulk Kill:** Single-click eradication for all detected threats matching or exceeding threat thresholds.\n64: * **Core OS Protection Whitelist:** Automatically protects critical Windows OS components (`smss.exe`, `csrss.exe`, `lsass.exe`, `services.exe` in `C:\\Windows\\System32`) from accidental termination.\n65: * **Threat Quarantine:** Isolates suspicious binaries into `quarantine/` with execution rights revoked and forensic receipts.\n66: * **Persistence Neutralization:** Scans and disables malicious autorun items in Windows Startup folders, Registry Run keys (`HKCU`/`HKLM`), and Scheduled Tasks.\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 🚀 Quick Start Guide\n71: \n72: ### Option 1: Double-Click Launcher (Windows)\n73: Double-click:\n74: ```bat\n75: start_dashboard.bat\n76: ```\n77: This automatically starts the backend server and launches the Web Dashboard at:\n78: 👉 **`http://127.0.0.1:8888`**\n79: \n80: ---\n81: \n82: ### Option 2: Command Line (CLI)\n83: \n84: #### 1. Start Web Dashboard\n85: ```bash\n86: python run_server.py\n87: ```\n88: \n89: #### 2. Terminal CLI Scanner\n90: ```bash\n91: # 1. Run deep process threat scan\n92: python cli_scanner.py --scan\n93: \n94: # 2. Deep scan and automatically kill all detected threats\n95: python cli_scanner.py --scan --kill\n96: \n97: # 3. Audit processes using network sockets and trace IP origins\n98: python cli_scanner.py --network\n99: \n100: # 4. View active allowlist and blocklist rules","truncated_fields":["content"]}
